
from typing import List, Dict, Tuple
from models import TranscriptEntry, TranscriptInput, SummarySegment, LLMOutput

val: List[TranscriptEntry] = [TranscriptEntry(start=0.0, text='If you feed a large language model the phrase, Michael Jordan plays the sport of blank,'), TranscriptEntry(start=5.038, text='and you have it predict what comes next, and it correctly predicts basketball,'), TranscriptEntry(start=9.56, text='this would suggest that somewhere, inside its hundreds of billions of parameters,'), TranscriptEntry(start=14.255, text="it's baked in knowledge about a specific person and his specific sport."), TranscriptEntry(start=18.94, text="And I think in general, anyone who's played around with one of these"), TranscriptEntry(start=22.146, text="models has the clear sense that it's memorized tons and tons of facts."), TranscriptEntry(start=25.7, text='So a reasonable question you could ask is, how exactly does that work?'), TranscriptEntry(start=29.16, text='And where do those facts live?'), TranscriptEntry(start=35.72, text='Last December, a few researchers from Google DeepMind posted about work on this question,'), TranscriptEntry(start=40.385, text='and they were using this specific example of matching athletes to their sports.'), TranscriptEntry(start=44.9, text='And although a full mechanistic understanding of how facts are stored remains unsolved,'), TranscriptEntry(start=49.824, text='they had some interesting partial results, including the very general high-level'), TranscriptEntry(start=54.357, text='conclusion that the facts seem to live inside a specific part of these networks,'), TranscriptEntry(start=58.89, text='known fancifully as the multi-layer perceptrons, or MLPs for short.'), TranscriptEntry(start=63.12, text='In the last couple of chapters, you and I have been digging into'), TranscriptEntry(start=66.262, text='the details behind transformers, the architecture underlying large language models,'), TranscriptEntry(start=70.324, text='and also underlying a lot of other modern AI.'), TranscriptEntry(start=73.06, text='In the most recent chapter, we were focusing on a piece called Attention.'), TranscriptEntry(start=76.84, text='And the next step for you and me is to dig into the details of what happens inside'), TranscriptEntry(start=80.964, text='these multi-layer perceptrons, which make up the other big portion of the network.'), TranscriptEntry(start=85.68, text='The computation here is actually relatively simple,'), TranscriptEntry(start=88.074, text='especially when you compare it to attention.'), TranscriptEntry(start=90.56, text='It boils down essentially to a pair of matrix'), TranscriptEntry(start=92.656, text='multiplications with a simple something in between.'), TranscriptEntry(start=95.72, text='However, interpreting what these computations are doing is exceedingly challenging.'), TranscriptEntry(start=101.56, text='Our main goal here is to step through the computations and make them memorable,'), TranscriptEntry(start=105.666, text="but I'd like to do it in the context of showing a specific example of how"), TranscriptEntry(start=109.464, text='one of these blocks could, at least in principle, store a concrete fact.'), TranscriptEntry(start=113.58, text="Specifically, it'll be storing the fact that Michael Jordan plays basketball."), TranscriptEntry(start=118.08, text='I should mention the layout here is inspired by a conversation'), TranscriptEntry(start=120.768, text='I had with one of those DeepMind researchers, Neil Nanda.'), TranscriptEntry(start=124.06, text="For the most part, I will assume that you've either watched the last two chapters,"), TranscriptEntry(start=128.038, text='or otherwise you have a basic sense for what a transformer is,'), TranscriptEntry(start=131.057, text="but refreshers never hurt, so here's the quick reminder of the overall flow."), TranscriptEntry(start=135.34, text="You and I have been studying a model that's trained"), TranscriptEntry(start=138.246, text='to take in a piece of text and predict what comes next.'), TranscriptEntry(start=141.72, text='That input text is first broken into a bunch of tokens,'), TranscriptEntry(start=144.965, text='which means little chunks that are typically words or little pieces of words,'), TranscriptEntry(start=149.485, text='and each token is associated with a high-dimensional vector,'), TranscriptEntry(start=153.02, text='which is to say a long list of numbers.'), TranscriptEntry(start=155.84, text='This sequence of vectors then repeatedly passes through two kinds of operation,'), TranscriptEntry(start=160.318, text='attention, which allows the vectors to pass information between one another,'), TranscriptEntry(start=164.629, text="and then the multilayer perceptrons, the thing that we're gonna dig into today,"), TranscriptEntry(start=169.108, text="and also there's a certain normalization step in between."), TranscriptEntry(start=173.3, text='After the sequence of vectors has flowed through many,'), TranscriptEntry(start=176.431, text='many different iterations of both of these blocks, by the end,'), TranscriptEntry(start=180.019, text='the hope is that each vector has soaked up enough information, both from the context,'), TranscriptEntry(start=184.916, text='all of the other words in the input, and also from the general knowledge that'), TranscriptEntry(start=189.357, text='was baked into the model weights through training,'), TranscriptEntry(start=192.261, text='that it can be used to make a prediction of what token comes next.'), TranscriptEntry(start=196.86, text='One of the key ideas that I want you to have in your mind is that all of'), TranscriptEntry(start=200.682, text='these vectors live in a very, very high-dimensional space,'), TranscriptEntry(start=203.772, text='and when you think about that space, different directions can encode different'), TranscriptEntry(start=207.909, text='kinds of meaning.'), TranscriptEntry(start=210.12, text='So a very classic example that I like to refer back to is how if you look'), TranscriptEntry(start=214.096, text='at the embedding of woman and subtract the embedding of man,'), TranscriptEntry(start=217.374, text='and you take that little step and you add it to another masculine noun,'), TranscriptEntry(start=221.242, text='something like uncle, you land somewhere very,'), TranscriptEntry(start=223.768, text='very close to the corresponding feminine noun.'), TranscriptEntry(start=226.44, text='In this sense, this particular direction encodes gender information.'), TranscriptEntry(start=231.64, text='The idea is that many other distinct directions in this super high-dimensional'), TranscriptEntry(start=235.614, text='space could correspond to other features that the model might want to represent.'), TranscriptEntry(start=241.4, text="In a transformer, these vectors don't merely encode the meaning of a single word, though."), TranscriptEntry(start=246.68, text='As they flow through the network, they imbibe a much richer meaning based'), TranscriptEntry(start=250.988, text="on all the context around them, and also based on the model's knowledge."), TranscriptEntry(start=255.88, text='Ultimately, each one needs to encode something far,'), TranscriptEntry(start=258.506, text='far beyond the meaning of a single word, since it needs to be sufficient to'), TranscriptEntry(start=262.345, text='predict what will come next.'), TranscriptEntry(start=264.56, text="We've already seen how attention blocks let you incorporate context,"), TranscriptEntry(start=268.48, text='but a majority of the model parameters actually live inside the MLP blocks,'), TranscriptEntry(start=272.798, text='and one thought for what they might be doing is that they offer extra capacity'), TranscriptEntry(start=277.287, text='to store facts.'), TranscriptEntry(start=278.72, text='Like I said, the lesson here is gonna center on the concrete toy example'), TranscriptEntry(start=282.345, text='of how exactly it could store the fact that Michael Jordan plays basketball.'), TranscriptEntry(start=287.12, text='Now, this toy example is gonna require that you and I make'), TranscriptEntry(start=289.53, text='a couple of assumptions about that high-dimensional space.'), TranscriptEntry(start=292.36, text="First, we'll suppose that one of the directions represents the idea of a first name"), TranscriptEntry(start=296.991, text='Michael, and then another nearly perpendicular direction represents the idea of the'), TranscriptEntry(start=301.623, text='last name Jordan, and then yet a third direction will represent the idea of basketball.'), TranscriptEntry(start=307.4, text='So specifically, what I mean by this is if you look in the network and'), TranscriptEntry(start=311.121, text='you pluck out one of the vectors being processed,'), TranscriptEntry(start=313.742, text='if its dot product with this first name Michael direction is one,'), TranscriptEntry(start=317.202, text="that's what it would mean for the vector to be encoding the idea of a"), TranscriptEntry(start=320.872, text='person with that first name.'), TranscriptEntry(start=323.8, text='Otherwise, that dot product would be zero or negative,'), TranscriptEntry(start=326.143, text="meaning the vector doesn't really align with that direction."), TranscriptEntry(start=329.42, text="And for simplicity, let's completely ignore the very reasonable"), TranscriptEntry(start=332.217, text='question of what it might mean if that dot product was bigger than one.'), TranscriptEntry(start=336.2, text='Similarly, its dot product with these other directions would'), TranscriptEntry(start=339.831, text='tell you whether it represents the last name Jordan or basketball.'), TranscriptEntry(start=344.74, text="So let's say a vector is meant to represent the full name, Michael Jordan,"), TranscriptEntry(start=348.791, text='then its dot product with both of these directions would have to be one.'), TranscriptEntry(start=353.48, text='Since the text Michael Jordan spans two different tokens,'), TranscriptEntry(start=356.658, text='this would also mean we have to assume that an earlier attention block has successfully'), TranscriptEntry(start=361.48, text='passed information to the second of these two vectors so as to ensure that it can'), TranscriptEntry(start=365.973, text='encode both names.'), TranscriptEntry(start=367.94, text="With all of those as the assumptions, let's now dive into the meat of the lesson."), TranscriptEntry(start=371.88, text='What happens inside a multilayer perceptron?'), TranscriptEntry(start=377.1, text='You might think of this sequence of vectors flowing into the block, and remember,'), TranscriptEntry(start=381.366, text='each vector was originally associated with one of the tokens from the input text.'), TranscriptEntry(start=386.08, text="What's gonna happen is that each individual vector from that sequence"), TranscriptEntry(start=389.442, text="goes through a short series of operations, we'll unpack them in just a moment,"), TranscriptEntry(start=393.237, text="and at the end, we'll get another vector with the same dimension."), TranscriptEntry(start=396.88, text='That other vector is gonna get added to the original one that flowed in,'), TranscriptEntry(start=400.999, text='and that sum is the result flowing out.'), TranscriptEntry(start=403.72, text='This sequence of operations is something you apply to every vector in the sequence,'), TranscriptEntry(start=407.946, text='associated with every token in the input, and it all happens in parallel.'), TranscriptEntry(start=412.1, text="In particular, the vectors don't talk to each other in this step,"), TranscriptEntry(start=414.605, text="they're all kind of doing their own thing."), TranscriptEntry(start=416.72, text='And for you and me, that actually makes it a lot simpler,'), TranscriptEntry(start=419.349, text='because it means if we understand what happens to just one of the'), TranscriptEntry(start=422.342, text='vectors through this block, we effectively understand what happens to all of them.'), TranscriptEntry(start=427.1, text='When I say this block is gonna encode the fact that Michael Jordan plays basketball,'), TranscriptEntry(start=431.38, text='what I mean is that if a vector flows in that encodes first name Michael and last'), TranscriptEntry(start=435.509, text='name Jordan, then this sequence of computations will produce something that includes'), TranscriptEntry(start=439.789, text='that direction basketball, which is what will add on to the vector in that position.'), TranscriptEntry(start=445.6, text='The first step of this process looks like multiplying that vector by a very big matrix.'), TranscriptEntry(start=450.04, text='No surprises there, this is deep learning.'), TranscriptEntry(start=452.68, text="And this matrix, like all of the other ones we've seen,"), TranscriptEntry(start=455.235, text='is filled with model parameters that are learned from data,'), TranscriptEntry(start=457.973, text='which you might think of as a bunch of knobs and dials that get tweaked and'), TranscriptEntry(start=461.441, text='tuned to determine what the model behavior is.'), TranscriptEntry(start=464.5, text='Now, one nice way to think about matrix multiplication is to imagine each row of'), TranscriptEntry(start=468.678, text='that matrix as being its own vector, and taking a bunch of dot products between'), TranscriptEntry(start=472.804, text="those rows and the vector being processed, which I'll label as E for embedding."), TranscriptEntry(start=477.28, text='For example, suppose that very first row happened to equal'), TranscriptEntry(start=480.576, text="this first name Michael direction that we're presuming exists."), TranscriptEntry(start=484.32, text='That would mean that the first component in this output, this dot product right here,'), TranscriptEntry(start=489.411, text='would be one if that vector encodes the first name Michael,'), TranscriptEntry(start=492.964, text='and zero or negative otherwise.'), TranscriptEntry(start=495.88, text='Even more fun, take a moment to think about what it would mean if that'), TranscriptEntry(start=499.505, text='first row was this first name Michael plus last name Jordan direction.'), TranscriptEntry(start=503.7, text='And for simplicity, let me go ahead and write that down as M plus J.'), TranscriptEntry(start=508.08, text='Then, taking a dot product with this embedding E,'), TranscriptEntry(start=510.931, text='things distribute really nicely, so it looks like M dot E plus J dot E.'), TranscriptEntry(start=514.98, text='And notice how that means the ultimate value would be two if the vector encodes the'), TranscriptEntry(start=519.782, text='full name Michael Jordan, and otherwise it would be one or something smaller than one.'), TranscriptEntry(start=525.34, text="And that's just one row in this matrix."), TranscriptEntry(start=527.6, text='You might think of all of the other rows as in parallel asking some other kinds of'), TranscriptEntry(start=531.871, text='questions, probing at some other sorts of features of the vector being processed.'), TranscriptEntry(start=536.7, text='Very often this step also involves adding another vector to the output,'), TranscriptEntry(start=539.916, text='which is full of model parameters learned from data.'), TranscriptEntry(start=542.24, text='This other vector is known as the bias.'), TranscriptEntry(start=545.18, text='For our example, I want you to imagine that the value of this'), TranscriptEntry(start=548.567, text='bias in that very first component is negative one,'), TranscriptEntry(start=551.353, text='meaning our final output looks like that relevant dot product, but minus one.'), TranscriptEntry(start=556.12, text='You might very reasonably ask why I would want you to assume that the'), TranscriptEntry(start=559.991, text="model has learned this, and in a moment you'll see why it's very clean"), TranscriptEntry(start=563.918, text='and nice if we have a value here which is positive if and only if a vector'), TranscriptEntry(start=568.067, text="encodes the full name Michael Jordan, and otherwise it's zero or negative."), TranscriptEntry(start=573.04, text='The total number of rows in this matrix, which is something'), TranscriptEntry(start=576.268, text='like the number of questions being asked, in the case of GPT-3,'), TranscriptEntry(start=579.712, text="whose numbers we've been following, is just under 50,000."), TranscriptEntry(start=583.1, text="In fact, it's exactly four times the number of dimensions in this embedding space."), TranscriptEntry(start=586.92, text="That's a design choice."), TranscriptEntry(start=587.94, text='You could make it more, you could make it less,'), TranscriptEntry(start=589.816, text='but having a clean multiple tends to be friendly for hardware.'), TranscriptEntry(start=592.74, text='Since this matrix full of weights maps us into a higher dimensional space,'), TranscriptEntry(start=596.945, text="I'm gonna give it the shorthand W up."), TranscriptEntry(start=599.02, text="I'll continue labeling the vector we're processing as E,"), TranscriptEntry(start=602.334, text="and let's label this bias vector as B up and put that all back down in the diagram."), TranscriptEntry(start=609.18, text='At this point, a problem is that this operation is purely linear,'), TranscriptEntry(start=612.956, text='but language is a very non-linear process.'), TranscriptEntry(start=615.88, text="If the entry that we're measuring is high for Michael plus Jordan,"), TranscriptEntry(start=619.778, text='it would also necessarily be somewhat triggered by Michael plus Phelps'), TranscriptEntry(start=623.91, text='and also Alexis plus Jordan, despite those being unrelated conceptually.'), TranscriptEntry(start=628.54, text='What you really want is a simple yes or no for the full name.'), TranscriptEntry(start=632.9, text='So the next step is to pass this large intermediate'), TranscriptEntry(start=635.443, text='vector through a very simple non-linear function.'), TranscriptEntry(start=638.36, text='A common choice is one that takes all of the negative values and'), TranscriptEntry(start=641.803, text='maps them to zero and leaves all of the positive values unchanged.'), TranscriptEntry(start=646.44, text='And continuing with the deep learning tradition of overly fancy names,'), TranscriptEntry(start=650.744, text='this very simple function is often called the rectified linear unit, or ReLU for short.'), TranscriptEntry(start=656.02, text="Here's what the graph looks like."), TranscriptEntry(start=658.3, text='So taking our imagined example where this first entry of the intermediate vector is one,'), TranscriptEntry(start=663.372, text='if and only if the full name is Michael Jordan and zero or negative otherwise,'), TranscriptEntry(start=667.874, text='after you pass it through the ReLU, you end up with a very clean value where'), TranscriptEntry(start=672.263, text='all of the zero and negative values just get clipped to zero.'), TranscriptEntry(start=676.1, text='So this output would be one for the full name Michael Jordan and zero otherwise.'), TranscriptEntry(start=680.56, text='In other words, it very directly mimics the behavior of an AND gate.'), TranscriptEntry(start=685.66, text="Often models will use a slightly modified function that's called the GELU,"), TranscriptEntry(start=689.252, text="which has the same basic shape, it's just a bit smoother."), TranscriptEntry(start=692.5, text="But for our purposes, it's a little bit cleaner if we only think about the ReLU."), TranscriptEntry(start=696.74, text='Also, when you hear people refer to the neurons of a transformer,'), TranscriptEntry(start=700.146, text="they're talking about these values right here."), TranscriptEntry(start=702.9, text='Whenever you see that common neural network picture with a layer of dots and a'), TranscriptEntry(start=707.39, text='bunch of lines connecting to the previous layer, which we had earlier in this series,'), TranscriptEntry(start=712.278, text="that's typically meant to convey this combination of a linear step,"), TranscriptEntry(start=716.144, text='a matrix multiplication, followed by some simple term-wise nonlinear function like a ReLU.'), TranscriptEntry(start=722.5, text='You would say that this neuron is active whenever this value'), TranscriptEntry(start=725.818, text="is positive and that it's inactive if that value is zero."), TranscriptEntry(start=730.12, text='The next step looks very similar to the first one.'), TranscriptEntry(start=732.56, text='You multiply by a very large matrix and you add on a certain bias term.'), TranscriptEntry(start=736.98, text='In this case, the number of dimensions in the output is back down to the size of'), TranscriptEntry(start=741.147, text="that embedding space, so I'm gonna go ahead and call this the down projection matrix."), TranscriptEntry(start=746.22, text='And this time, instead of thinking of things row by row,'), TranscriptEntry(start=748.907, text="it's actually nicer to think of it column by column."), TranscriptEntry(start=751.86, text='You see, another way that you can hold matrix multiplication in your head is to'), TranscriptEntry(start=756.252, text='imagine taking each column of the matrix and multiplying it by the corresponding'), TranscriptEntry(start=760.698, text="term in the vector that it's processing and adding together all of those rescaled columns."), TranscriptEntry(start=766.84, text="The reason it's nicer to think about this way is because here the columns have the same"), TranscriptEntry(start=771.361, text='dimension as the embedding space, so we can think of them as directions in that space.'), TranscriptEntry(start=776.14, text='For instance, we will imagine that the model has learned to make that'), TranscriptEntry(start=779.685, text='first column into this basketball direction that we suppose exists.'), TranscriptEntry(start=784.18, text='What that would mean is that when the relevant neuron in that first position is active,'), TranscriptEntry(start=788.45, text="we'll be adding this column to the final result."), TranscriptEntry(start=791.14, text='But if that neuron was inactive, if that number was zero, then this would have no effect.'), TranscriptEntry(start=796.5, text="And it doesn't just have to be basketball."), TranscriptEntry(start=798.22, text='The model could also bake into this column and many other features that'), TranscriptEntry(start=801.638, text='it wants to associate with something that has the full name Michael Jordan.'), TranscriptEntry(start=806.98, text='And at the same time, all of the other columns in this matrix are telling you'), TranscriptEntry(start=811.851, text='what will be added to the final result if the corresponding neuron is active.'), TranscriptEntry(start=817.36, text="And if you have a bias in this case, it's something that you're"), TranscriptEntry(start=820.454, text='just adding every single time, regardless of the neuron values.'), TranscriptEntry(start=824.06, text="You might wonder what's that doing."), TranscriptEntry(start=825.54, text="As with all parameter-filled objects here, it's kind of hard to say exactly."), TranscriptEntry(start=829.32, text="Maybe there's some bookkeeping that the network needs to do,"), TranscriptEntry(start=832.287, text='but you can feel free to ignore it for now.'), TranscriptEntry(start=834.86, text='Making our notation a little more compact again,'), TranscriptEntry(start=837.738, text="I'll call this big matrix W down and similarly call that bias vector B down and"), TranscriptEntry(start=842.438, text='put that back into our diagram.'), TranscriptEntry(start=844.74, text='Like I previewed earlier, what you do with this final result is add it to the vector'), TranscriptEntry(start=849.118, text='that flowed into the block at that position and that gets you this final result.'), TranscriptEntry(start=853.82, text='So for example, if the vector flowing in encoded both first name Michael and last name'), TranscriptEntry(start=859.06, text='Jordan, then because this sequence of operations will trigger that AND gate,'), TranscriptEntry(start=863.698, text='it will add on the basketball direction, so what pops out will encode all of those'), TranscriptEntry(start=868.697, text='together.'), TranscriptEntry(start=869.82, text='And remember, this is a process happening to every one of those vectors in parallel.'), TranscriptEntry(start=874.8, text="In particular, taking the GPT-3 numbers, it means that this block doesn't just"), TranscriptEntry(start=879.767, text='have 50,000 neurons in it, it has 50,000 times the number of tokens in the input.'), TranscriptEntry(start=888.18, text='So that is the entire operation, two matrix products,'), TranscriptEntry(start=891.356, text='each with a bias added and a simple clipping function in between.'), TranscriptEntry(start=896.08, text='Any of you who watched the earlier videos of the series will recognize this'), TranscriptEntry(start=899.415, text='structure as the most basic kind of neural network that we studied there.'), TranscriptEntry(start=903.08, text='In that example, it was trained to recognize handwritten digits.'), TranscriptEntry(start=906.58, text='Over here, in the context of a transformer for a large language model,'), TranscriptEntry(start=910.804, text='this is one piece in a larger architecture and any attempt to interpret'), TranscriptEntry(start=915.088, text="what exactly it's doing is heavily intertwined with the idea of encoding"), TranscriptEntry(start=919.431, text='information into vectors of a high-dimensional embedding space.'), TranscriptEntry(start=924.26, text='That is the core lesson, but I do wanna step back and reflect on two different things,'), TranscriptEntry(start=928.6, text='the first of which is a kind of bookkeeping, and the second of which'), TranscriptEntry(start=932.043, text='involves a very thought-provoking fact about higher dimensions that'), TranscriptEntry(start=935.435, text="I actually didn't know until I dug into transformers."), TranscriptEntry(start=941.08, text='In the last two chapters, you and I started counting up the total number of parameters'), TranscriptEntry(start=945.947, text="in GPT-3 and seeing exactly where they live, so let's quickly finish up the game here."), TranscriptEntry(start=951.4, text='I already mentioned how this up projection matrix has just under 50,000 rows and'), TranscriptEntry(start=956.79, text='that each row matches the size of the embedding space, which for GPT-3 is 12,288.'), TranscriptEntry(start=963.24, text='Multiplying those together, it gives us 604 million parameters just for that matrix,'), TranscriptEntry(start=968.517, text='and the down projection has the same number of parameters just with a transposed shape.'), TranscriptEntry(start=974.5, text='So together, they give about 1.2 billion parameters.'), TranscriptEntry(start=978.28, text='The bias vector also accounts for a couple more parameters,'), TranscriptEntry(start=980.885, text="but it's a trivial proportion of the total, so I'm not even gonna show it."), TranscriptEntry(start=984.66, text='In GPT-3, this sequence of embedding vectors flows through not one,'), TranscriptEntry(start=989.612, text='but 96 distinct MLPs, so the total number of parameters devoted'), TranscriptEntry(start=994.273, text='to all of these blocks adds up to about 116 billion.'), TranscriptEntry(start=998.82, text='This is around 2 thirds of the total parameters in the network,'), TranscriptEntry(start=1002.177, text='and when you add it to everything that we had before, for the attention blocks,'), TranscriptEntry(start=1006.374, text='the embedding, and the unembedding, you do indeed get that grand total of 175'), TranscriptEntry(start=1010.465, text='billion as advertised.'), TranscriptEntry(start=1013.06, text="It's probably worth mentioning there's another set of parameters associated"), TranscriptEntry(start=1016.637, text='with those normalization steps that this explanation has skipped over,'), TranscriptEntry(start=1019.979, text='but like the bias vector, they account for a very trivial proportion of the total.'), TranscriptEntry(start=1025.9, text='As to that second point of reflection, you might be wondering if'), TranscriptEntry(start=1029.16, text="this central toy example we've been spending so much time on"), TranscriptEntry(start=1032.219, text='reflects how facts are actually stored in real large language models.'), TranscriptEntry(start=1036.319, text='It is true that the rows of that first matrix can be thought of as'), TranscriptEntry(start=1039.768, text='directions in this embedding space, and that means the activation of each'), TranscriptEntry(start=1043.576, text='neuron tells you how much a given vector aligns with some specific direction.'), TranscriptEntry(start=1047.76, text="It's also true that the columns of that second matrix tell"), TranscriptEntry(start=1050.968, text='you what will be added to the result if that neuron is active.'), TranscriptEntry(start=1054.64, text='Both of those are just mathematical facts.'), TranscriptEntry(start=1057.74, text='However, the evidence does suggest that individual neurons very rarely'), TranscriptEntry(start=1061.806, text='represent a single clean feature like Michael Jordan,'), TranscriptEntry(start=1064.899, text='and there may actually be a very good reason this is the case,'), TranscriptEntry(start=1068.507, text='related to an idea floating around interpretability researchers these'), TranscriptEntry(start=1072.516, text='days known as superposition.'), TranscriptEntry(start=1074.64, text='This is a hypothesis that might help to explain both why the models are'), TranscriptEntry(start=1078.557, text='especially hard to interpret and also why they scale surprisingly well.'), TranscriptEntry(start=1083.5, text='The basic idea is that if you have an n-dimensional space and you wanna'), TranscriptEntry(start=1087.386, text='represent a bunch of different features using directions that are all'), TranscriptEntry(start=1091.165, text='perpendicular to one another in that space, you know,'), TranscriptEntry(start=1094.08, text='that way if you add a component in one direction,'), TranscriptEntry(start=1096.78, text="it doesn't influence any of the other directions,"), TranscriptEntry(start=1099.479, text='then the maximum number of vectors you can fit is only n, the number of dimensions.'), TranscriptEntry(start=1104.6, text='To a mathematician, actually, this is the definition of dimension.'), TranscriptEntry(start=1108.22, text='But where it gets interesting is if you relax that'), TranscriptEntry(start=1110.873, text='constraint a little bit and you tolerate some noise.'), TranscriptEntry(start=1114.18, text="Say you allow those features to be represented by vectors that aren't exactly"), TranscriptEntry(start=1118.709, text="perpendicular, they're just nearly perpendicular, maybe between 89 and 91 degrees apart."), TranscriptEntry(start=1124.82, text='If we were in two or three dimensions, this makes no difference.'), TranscriptEntry(start=1128.26, text='That gives you hardly any extra wiggle room to fit more vectors in,'), TranscriptEntry(start=1131.608, text='which makes it all the more counterintuitive that for higher dimensions,'), TranscriptEntry(start=1135.204, text='the answer changes dramatically.'), TranscriptEntry(start=1137.66, text='I can give you a really quick and dirty illustration of this using some'), TranscriptEntry(start=1141.845, text="scrappy Python that's going to create a list of 100-dimensional vectors,"), TranscriptEntry(start=1146.088, text='each one initialized randomly, and this list is going to contain 10,000 distinct vectors,'), TranscriptEntry(start=1151.319, text='so 100 times as many vectors as there are dimensions.'), TranscriptEntry(start=1155.32, text='This plot right here shows the distribution of angles between pairs of these vectors.'), TranscriptEntry(start=1160.68, text='So because they started at random, those angles could be anything from 0 to 180 degrees,'), TranscriptEntry(start=1165.393, text="but you'll notice that already, even just for random vectors,"), TranscriptEntry(start=1168.676, text="there's this heavy bias for things to be closer to 90 degrees."), TranscriptEntry(start=1172.5, text="Then what I'm going to do is run a certain optimization process that iteratively nudges"), TranscriptEntry(start=1177.169, text='all of these vectors so that they try to become more perpendicular to one another.'), TranscriptEntry(start=1182.06, text="After repeating this many different times, here's"), TranscriptEntry(start=1184.533, text='what the distribution of angles looks like.'), TranscriptEntry(start=1187.12, text='We have to actually zoom in on it here because all of the possible angles'), TranscriptEntry(start=1191.819, text='between pairs of vectors sit inside this narrow range between 89 and 91 degrees.'), TranscriptEntry(start=1198.02, text='In general, a consequence of something known as the Johnson-Lindenstrauss'), TranscriptEntry(start=1202.217, text='lemma is that the number of vectors you can cram into a space that are nearly'), TranscriptEntry(start=1206.642, text='perpendicular like this grows exponentially with the number of dimensions.'), TranscriptEntry(start=1211.96, text='This is very significant for large language models,'), TranscriptEntry(start=1214.82, text='which might benefit from associating independent ideas with nearly'), TranscriptEntry(start=1218.505, text='perpendicular directions.'), TranscriptEntry(start=1220.0, text="It means that it's possible for it to store many,"), TranscriptEntry(start=1222.596, text="many more ideas than there are dimensions in the space that it's allotted."), TranscriptEntry(start=1227.32, text='This might partially explain why model performance seems to scale so well with size.'), TranscriptEntry(start=1232.54, text='A space that has 10 times as many dimensions can store way,'), TranscriptEntry(start=1236.316, text='way more than 10 times as many independent ideas.'), TranscriptEntry(start=1240.42, text='And this is relevant not just to that embedding space where the vectors'), TranscriptEntry(start=1243.871, text='flowing through the model live, but also to that vector full of neurons'), TranscriptEntry(start=1247.323, text='in the middle of that multilayer perceptron that we just studied.'), TranscriptEntry(start=1250.96, text='That is to say, at the sizes of GPT-3, it might not just be probing at 50,000 features,'), TranscriptEntry(start=1256.113, text='but if it instead leveraged this enormous added capacity by using'), TranscriptEntry(start=1259.978, text='nearly perpendicular directions of the space, it could be probing at many,'), TranscriptEntry(start=1264.37, text='many more features of the vector being processed.'), TranscriptEntry(start=1267.78, text='But if it was doing that, what it means is that individual'), TranscriptEntry(start=1270.926, text="features aren't gonna be visible as a single neuron lighting up."), TranscriptEntry(start=1274.66, text='It would have to look like some specific combination of neurons instead, a superposition.'), TranscriptEntry(start=1280.4, text='For any of you curious to learn more, a key relevant search term here is sparse'), TranscriptEntry(start=1284.315, text='autoencoder, which is a tool that some of the interpretability people use to try to'), TranscriptEntry(start=1288.426, text="extract what the true features are, even if they're very superimposed on all these"), TranscriptEntry(start=1292.488, text='neurons.'), TranscriptEntry(start=1293.54, text="I'll link to a couple really great anthropic posts all about this."), TranscriptEntry(start=1297.88, text="At this point, we haven't touched every detail of a transformer,"), TranscriptEntry(start=1300.97, text='but you and I have hit the most important points.'), TranscriptEntry(start=1303.52, text='The main thing that I wanna cover in a next chapter is the training process.'), TranscriptEntry(start=1308.46, text="On the one hand, the short answer for how training works is that it's all"), TranscriptEntry(start=1311.929, text='backpropagation, and we covered backpropagation in a separate context with earlier'), TranscriptEntry(start=1315.821, text='chapters in the series.'), TranscriptEntry(start=1317.22, text='But there is more to discuss, like the specific cost function used for language models,'), TranscriptEntry(start=1322.034, text='the idea of fine-tuning using reinforcement learning with human feedback,'), TranscriptEntry(start=1326.083, text='and the notion of scaling laws.'), TranscriptEntry(start=1328.96, text='Quick note for the active followers among you,'), TranscriptEntry(start=1331.113, text="there are a number of non-machine learning-related videos that I'm excited to"), TranscriptEntry(start=1334.686, text='sink my teeth into before I make that next chapter, so it might be a while,'), TranscriptEntry(start=1338.167, text="but I do promise it'll come in due time."), TranscriptEntry(start=1355.64, text='Thank you.')]